t mutate case. Build internal data structures.\n\nSteps:\n- Create a dict mapping id to node object (original dict).\n- Determine children for each parent.\n- Build set of required symbols.\n- Determine initial set of retained nodes: all nodes that are not removable? Actually initially all nodes are present, and we will mark some as removed. We can maintain a set of removed_node_ids.\n- Process nodes in descending depth order. For equal depth, sort by node ID ascending? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Could be ascending order.\n- For each node in that sorted list:\n  - Skip if it's root (parent null) or its symbol is in required_symbols or it's not removable (by field).\n  - Also skip if the node has any children that are not removed (i.e., retained children). Since we are going deeper first, children might already be removed. But careful: after we process a node, we may later remove its parent if children are gone. So we need to check current retained children.\n  - If conditions met, tentatively remove: check that all required symbols are still represented in the retained (non-removed) nodes. If yes, then actually remove it: add to removed_node_ids list, and update removed set. If no, skip (retain it). \n- After processing all nodes, retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes not in removed set. Sort them (ascending order? The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\" - likely alphabetically ascending as string). Output as list of strings.\n\nEdge cases: multiple nodes with same symbol. required_symbols list contains symbol strings. A symbol is \"represented\" if there exists at least one node with that symbol that is not removed. Even if a node with that symbol is removed, if another node with same symbol remains, it's fine. So we need to count remaining nodes per symbol? Or just check existence.\n\nImplementation: maintain a dictionary of symbol->count of retained nodes. Initially compute from all nodes. When we remove a node, decrement count for its